Body:
I’d like to add some implementation detail and a concrete proposal that should make this easier to fix.

## Current behavior & root cause

In the web/desktop UI, all markdown headings (`h1`–`h6`) rendered inside chat messages are effectively styled as body text. They share:

- the same font size: `14px`
- the same color: `var(--text-strong)`
- only a marginally heavier font weight: `var(--font-weight-medium)` (500 vs 400 for body)

From `packages/ui/src/components/markdown.css`:

```css
/* Headings: Same size, distinguished by color and spacing */
h1,
h2,
h3,
h4,
h5,
h6 {
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--text-strong);
  font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  margin-top: 0px;
  margin-bottom: 24px;
  line-height: var(--line-height-large);
}
```

In practice:

- There is almost no visual hierarchy between heading levels.
- Headings are nearly indistinguishable from bold text inside messages.
- Long, structured responses (plans, summaries, multi-step guides) are hard to scan, even when the model is instructed to use markdown headings.

The theme system already defines a dedicated heading color token, but it’s not used here:

```css
/* packages/ui/src/styles/theme.css */

--font-size-small: 13px;
--font-size-base: 14px;
--font-size-large: 16px;
--font-size-x-large: 20px;
…
--markdown-heading: #9d7cd8;
```

So the root cause is:

1. All heading levels share the body font size.
2. They use the same color as body copy.
3. The existing `--markdown-heading` token is defined but unused.

## Proposed fix

Use the existing design tokens to create a clear but not overwhelming hierarchy for headings inside chat messages, and wire in `--markdown-heading` (with a safe fallback for custom themes).

Suggested change for `packages/ui/src/components/markdown.css`:

```css
/* Headings: scaled sizes with distinct color */
h1 {
  font-size: 20px; /* matches --font-size-x-large */
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--markdown-heading, var(--text-strong));
  margin-top: 16px;
  margin-bottom: 8px;
  line-height: var(--line-height-large);
}

h2 {
  font-size: 16px; /* matches --font-size-large */
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--markdown-heading, var(--text-strong));
  margin-top: 16px;
  margin-bottom: 8px;
  line-height: var(--line-height-large);
}

h3 {
  font-size: 14px; /* matches --font-size-base */
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--markdown-heading, var(--text-strong));
  margin-top: 12px;
  margin-bottom: 6px;
  line-height: var(--line-height-large);
}

h4,
h5,
h6 {
  font-size: 14px; /* base size, used mainly for hierarchy via weight/spacing */
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--markdown-heading, var(--text-strong));
  margin-top: 8px;
  margin-bottom: 4px;
  line-height: var(--line-height-large);
}
```

Notes:

- Font sizes map directly to existing tokens:  
  - `h1` → `--font-size-x-large` (20px)  
  - `h2` → `--font-size-large` (16px)  
  - `h3+` → `--font-size-base` (14px)
- `var(--markdown-heading, var(--text-strong))` keeps existing themes working and falls back cleanly for custom themes that don’t define `--markdown-heading`.
- Spacing is reduced and differentiated by level to keep the chat layout compact while still making sections scannable.

## workaround

My current  is to use the stylus browser extension and have it inject the following css to my opencode webui:

```css
[data-component="markdown"] h1 {
    font-size: 20px !important;
    font-weight: 700 !important;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
}
[data-component="markdown"] h2 {
    font-size: 17px !important;
    font-weight: 600 !important;
}
[data-component="markdown"] h3 {
    font-size: 15px !important;
    font-weight: 600 !important;
}
```

